From: Velocor <noreply@velocor.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] bdev/raid: add a background scrub

A raid bdev's copies are only read when a host reads them, and then only one of them, so a
latent sector error on another member surfaces during a rebuild, when it turns a degraded
array into a lost one. RAID1 also has no resync after an unclean shutdown: members can differ
where writes were in flight, and a read then returns either version depending on the read
balancer.

Add a scrub, started and stopped with bdev_raid_scrub_start / bdev_raid_scrub_stop and
reported under "scrub" by bdev_raid_get_bdevs. It runs on the app thread, window by window
(process_window_size_kb), over every operational base bdev's data area:

- RAID1: the window's LBA range of the raid bdev is quiesced (user I/O to it waits) and read
  from every member. A member whose read fails is rewritten from the first readable copy; the
  other copies are compared with it block by block, and differing blocks are counted and, in
  repair mode (the default), rewritten from it, like Linux md's "repair". A failed rewrite
  fails the member, like raid1's read-error correction. A window no member can read is counted
  as unrecoverable.
- Other levels: a surface scan of each member without quiescing; read errors are counted as
  unrecoverable.

It can be rate-limited (max_bandwidth_mb_sec) and started at an offset (to resume). It stops
after the current window on request, when a base bdev is removed (the removal's quiesce waits
for that window) or a rebuild starts (the target holds stale data beyond the rebuild window),
and destructing the raid bdev waits for it to stop. It is refused while a background process
runs and on raid bdevs with separate metadata.

Also: the schema entries, rpc.py commands and RPC documentation.

Unit tests: test_raid_scrub, test_raid_scrub_stops_for_rebuild (with memory-backed base bdevs),
test_raid_scrub_due_tsc (the rate limit past 64-bit products).

Applies to SPDK v26.09 (0bbb7fe4dccfb31e8d446da8822360704949d1e9) after 0001-0003.
